 "article": {
  "blocks": [
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "Parents in a rural Maryland community are rallying behind a school bus driver who was fired after she stopped to let a student rescue an injured kitten."
   },
   {
    "type": "Media",
    "mediaId": "amWqz1j",
    "caption": "Jarrettsville Vet Center/Facebook"
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "Cynthia Morgan, 67, worked as a school bus driver in the Harford County Public Schools district for 11 years. She was recently removed from her route after she let a North Harford Elementary School student get off the bus to pick up an injured kitten. Morgan says being fired devastated her."
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "Parents like Carolyn Kalambihis say they understand the safety concerns but believe removing Morgan from her route was excessive. Video recorded on the rural Heaps School Road shows little traffic, a detail parents say is important to understanding what happened."
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "Per [KLTV](https://www.kltv.com/2026/10/11/longtime-school-bus-driver-fired-after-stopping-let-student-rescue-injured-kitten/), Kalambihis said:"
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "> We live in North Harford, so it’s very rural back here. She wouldn’t have done it, you know, let a child get off the bus to get a kitten if it was a more high-traffic road."
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "She says Morgan has been driving her kids to school for five years, and they absolutely love her."
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "Kalambihis added:"
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "> She is an angel. She’s the most caring person. Every year at the beginning of the school year, she buys every child on the bus school supplies. She buys them gifts for Christmas, every holiday, birthdays. She’s given them birthday cards, all out of her own pocket."
   },
   {
    "type": "Media",
    "mediaId": "aZnqpw0",
    "caption": "(via Getty Images)"
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "Now, Kalambihis and other Harford County parents are circulating a [petition](https://www.change.org/p/reinstate-cynthia-morgan-as-north-harford-bus-driver) that calls for Morgan to be allowed to return to her route."
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "She said:"
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "> We just feel the way that it was handled was not appropriate. We feel that termination was a little excessive, an overreaction by the Department of Transportation through HCPS. I think it could have been handled in a lot of other ways."
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "Kalambihis said she believes Morgan understands that allowing a student off the bus was a mistake."
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "> It’s definitely a concern for safety. There is no question that it was a mistake. It shouldn’t have happened. I believe she knows that and understands and, looking back, knows she made a mistake and should not have done what she did."
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "Harford County Public Schools said Morgan was employed by an independent contractor, not directly by the school district. However, the district is responsible for certifying drivers who transport students for its schools."
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "The district said in a statement obtained by [CBS News](https://www.cbsnews.com/baltimore/news/harford-county-parents-school-bus-driver-kitten-maryland/):"
   },
   {
    "type": "RichText",
    "content": "> Certification and decertification are not always based on a single factor but often involve consideration of multiple circumstances and requirements. Student safety remains our top priority, and all certification decisions are made with that responsibility in mind."
   },
   {
    "type": "Media",
    "mediaId": "avW2neO",
    "caption": "WJZ/YouTube"
   },
   {
